//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// The three cases where a host is *not* required (#341). The fourth -- staging
// through host memory against a machine model with no host named -- is
// tests/frontend/fail/host_not_declared.vx.
//
// The rule has to be narrow or it is noise. `--host` exists because no host is
// assumed, not because every compilation is a fleet question.
//
// 1. A native build needs no host, and is not testable *here*: with no machine
//    model there is no `Memory::HBM` to transfer into either, so this program
//    fails for an unrelated reason. The property is real and is demonstrated by
//    the rest of the suite, every file of which compiles with neither flag.
//
// 2. `--host default`: the machine compiling the program, declared rather than
//    assumed. Synthesised rather than read from a file, because for a native
//    host the statement *is* "this one".
// RUN: vxc %s --machine fleet/a100-80.vx --host default --emit-mlir 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=DEFAULT
// DEFAULT-NOT: E6014
//
// 3. A host file: a peer module the program never names, so the same text
//    compiles against a different host by swapping it. It declares `CPU_DRAM`
//    and nothing else -- passing a *machine* file here collides on `HBM` and
//    `Topology Device` (E6012), which is the system saying that a file
//    describing a device is describing a device.
// RUN: vxc %s --machine fleet/a100-80.vx --host fleet/host-x86-e5-2666v3.vx --emit-mlir 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FILE
// FILE-NOT: E6014
//
// And the transfer still lowers to the plugin either way -- requiring a host
// changed what the compiler insists on knowing, not what it emits.
// FILE: vx.transfer

fn main() -> i32 {
  let mut weights = Tensor<f32>([ 64, 64 ]);
  let weights_d = transfer(weights, Memory::HBM);
  return 0;
}
